/* Detailed worked solutions. Mathematical expressions use MathJax delimiters. */
const SR=String.raw;
const DS=(answer,...steps)=>`<b>Answer ${answer}.</b><ol class="worked-steps">${steps.map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join('')}</ol>`;
function applyDetailedPracticeSolutions(questions,solutions){
 if(questions.length!==solutions.length)throw Error('Incomplete worked solutions');
 questions.forEach((q,i)=>{if(!solutions[i].startsWith(`<b>Answer ${'ABCDEFGHIJ'[q.correct]}.</b>`))throw Error('Solution/key mismatch: Q'+q.n);q.sol=solutions[i];});
}
const DETAILED_SET_A_P1=[
DS('C',SR`Both square roots must exist, so \(-4\le x\le9\). Squaring the equation gives \((x+4)+(9-x)+2\sqrt{(x+4)(9-x)}=25\). Thus \(13+2\sqrt{(x+4)(9-x)}=25\), and the remaining square root is 6.`,SR`Square again: \((x+4)(9-x)=36\). Expanding gives \(-x^2+5x+36=36\), so \(x(5-x)=0\). The only candidates are \(x=0\) and \(x=5\).`,SR`Check the original equation, since squaring can introduce unwanted roots. At 0 the left side is \(\sqrt4+\sqrt9=2+3=5\); at 5 it is \(\sqrt9+\sqrt4=3+2=5\). Both are in the domain. There are exactly two solutions.`),
DS('A',SR`Combine the two eighth powers: \((1-x)^8(1+x+x^2)^8=[(1-x)(1+x+x^2)]^8\). Multiply the factors: \(1+x+x^2-x-x^2-x^3=1-x^3\). The expression is therefore \((1-x^3)^8\).`,SR`In the binomial expansion, choosing \(-x^3\) from \(j\) of the eight factors contributes \(\binom8j(-1)^jx^{3j}\). To obtain \(x^{18}\), we need \(3j=18\), so \(j=6\).`,SR`The required coefficient is \(\binom86(-1)^6=\binom82=8\cdot7/(2\cdot1)=28\). The sign is positive because six is even.`),
DS('D',SR`Mean equals total divided by number of values. Hence the original total is \(6\times10=60\). After the largest value is removed, the total is \(5\times8=40\), so the largest value is \(60-40=20\).`,SR`Removing the smallest value instead leaves total \(5\times11=55\). The smallest value is therefore \(60-55=5\).`,SR`The range is largest minus smallest: \(20-5=15\). The two removals are separate experiments on the original data; they are not successive removals.`),
DS('B',SR`Let the arithmetic progression have first term \(a\) and common difference \(d\). Its 2nd, 5th and 11th terms are \(a+d,a+4d,a+10d\). Their consecutive differences are \(3d\) and \(6d\).`,SR`Write the three geometric terms as \(u,ur,ur^2\). Their consecutive differences are \(u(r-1)\) and \(ur(r-1)\). Since the progression is non-constant, \(u\ne0\), \(r\ne1\), and consequently \(d\ne0\). We may divide the second difference by the first.`,SR`For the arithmetic expressions this ratio is \(6d/(3d)=2\). For the geometric expressions it is \(ur(r-1)/(u(r-1))=r\). Equating gives \(r=2\).`),
DS('E',SR`Use the complementary-angle relation \(\cos3x=\sin(\pi/2-3x)\). The sine graph has equal values at angles that differ by a full turn, or at supplementary angles, allowing full turns in either case. Thus \(\sin u=\sin v\) means \(u=v+2k\pi\) or \(u=\pi-v+2k\pi\), for an integer \(k\).`,SR`The first family gives \(2x=\pi/2-3x+2k\pi\), so \(x=\pi/10+2k\pi/5\). The values in \([0,2\pi)\) occur for \(k=0,1,2,3,4\): \(\pi/10,\pi/2,9\pi/10,13\pi/10,17\pi/10\).`,SR`The second family gives \(2x=\pi-(\pi/2-3x)+2k\pi\), so \(x=-\pi/2-2k\pi\). Only \(k=-1\) puts this in the interval, giving \(3\pi/2\). This is not in the first list. The total is therefore \(5+1=6\).`),
DS('A',SR`All \(6^3=216\) ordered dice outcomes are equally likely. After conditioning on a valid triangle, each valid ordered outcome remains equally likely. Sort a triangle's sides as \(a\le b\le c\). The only triangle inequality that needs checking is \(a+b>c\).`,SR`For three distinct sides, list the sorted possibilities by largest side. For \(c=3\): \((2,3)\) cannot occur as the smaller pair, so there are none; directly the only triple \((1,2,3)\) is degenerate. For \(c=4\), the smaller pair is \((2,3)\). For \(c=5\), the pairs are \((2,4),(3,4)\). For \(c=6\), the pairs are \((2,5),(3,4),(3,5),(4,5)\). Thus there are \(1+2+4=7\) sorted distinct triangles, each with six orderings: 42 outcomes.`,SR`For exactly two equal sides, write the sides as \((a,a,b)\), with \(b\ne a\) and \(b<2a\). For \(a=1,2,3,4,5,6\), the permitted counts of \(b\) are respectively \(0,2,4,5,5,5\). Their sum is 21, and each has three orderings, giving 63 outcomes. All three equal gives another six outcomes.`,SR`The number of valid outcomes is \(42+63+6=111\). Hence the conditional probability is \(42/111=14/37\). Equality \(a+b=c\) is excluded because it gives a straight line, not a triangle.`),
DS('C',SR`Factor the expression: \(x^3-x=x(x-1)(x+1)\). Its zeros in the integration interval are \(-1,0,1\). Its sign is positive on \((-1,0)\), negative on \((0,1)\), and positive on \((1,2)\).`,SR`Therefore the integral of the absolute value is \(\int_{-1}^{0}(x^3-x)\,dx-\int_0^1(x^3-x)\,dx+\int_1^2(x^3-x)\,dx\). An antiderivative is \(F(x)=x^4/4-x^2/2\).`,SR`The endpoint values are \(F(-1)=-1/4\), \(F(0)=0\), \(F(1)=-1/4\), and \(F(2)=4-2=2\). Hence the three positive contributions are \(1/4,1/4,2-(-1/4)=9/4\). Their sum is \(11/4\).`),
DS('B',SR`Rewrite the numerator as \((n+1)^2+2025\). The fraction is therefore \(n+1+2025/(n+1)\). The first term is an integer, so the fraction is an integer exactly when \(n+1\) is a positive divisor of 2025.`,SR`Factorise \(2025=81\times25=3^4 5^2\). Every positive divisor is \(3^i5^j\), with \(i=0,1,2,3,4\) and \(j=0,1,2\). These independent choices give \(5\times3=15\) divisors.`,SR`But \(n\) is positive, so \(n+1\ge2\). The divisor 1 would give \(n=0\) and must be removed. The answer is \(15-1=14\).`),
DS('D',SR`At an intersection with \(y=1\), we have \(3-f(2x-1)=1\), which rearranges to \(f(2x-1)=2\). Let the three distinct solutions of \(f(u)=2\) be \(u_1,u_2,u_3\).`,SR`For each one, \(2x-1=u_i\) gives exactly one value \(x=(u_i+1)/2\). Distinct values of \(u_i\) give distinct values of \(x\), because this linear transformation is reversible.`,SR`There are consequently exactly three intersections. Horizontal stretching, compression or reflection cannot merge these distinct inputs under a non-zero linear scale factor.`),
DS('E',SR`Put \(u=2x-y\) and \(v=x+2y\). Expanding gives \(u^2+v^2=(4x^2-4xy+y^2)+(x^2+4xy+4y^2)=5(x^2+y^2)=25\). The quantity to maximise is \(uv\).`,SR`Since \((u-v)^2\ge0\), we have \(u^2+v^2-2uv\ge0\). Thus \(25-2uv\ge0\), so \(uv\le25/2\).`,SR`Equality requires \(u=v\), or \(2x-y=x+2y\), hence \(x=3y\). Substitute into \(x^2+y^2=5\): \(10y^2=5\), giving \(y=\pm1/\sqrt2\) and \(x=\pm3/\sqrt2\) with matching signs. These are allowed, so the upper bound \(25/2\) is attained.`),
DS('B',SR`If the radius is \(r>0\), tangency to both axes in the first quadrant puts the centre at \((r,r)\). The circle is \((x-r)^2+(y-r)^2=r^2\). A point on the line has \(y=6-x/2\).`,SR`Substitute the line into the circle: \((x-r)^2+(6-x/2-r)^2=r^2\). Expanding gives \(x^2-2rx+r^2+(6-r)^2-(6-r)x+x^2/4=r^2\). Cancel \(r^2\) and collect terms to obtain \(\tfrac54x^2-(r+6)x+(r-6)^2=0\).`,SR`Tangency means exactly one intersection, so the discriminant is zero: \((r+6)^2-5(r-6)^2=0\). Expanding gives \(r^2+12r+36-5r^2+60r-180=0\), or \(-4r^2+72r-144=0\). Divide by -4: \(r^2-18r+36=0\).`,SR`The roots are \([18\pm\sqrt{324-144}]/2=9\pm3\sqrt5\). Both are positive since \(9>3\sqrt5\). Thus both correspond to circles as specified, and their sum is \(18\).`),
DS('A',SR`The tangent gradient at \((t,t^2)\) is \(2t\), so the normal gradient is \(-1/(2t)\); this is defined because \(t>0\). The normal has equation \(y-t^2=-(x-t)/(2t)\).`,SR`At another intersection with \(y=x^2\), substitute and factor: \(x^2-t^2=-(x-t)/(2t)\), so \((x-t)[x+t+1/(2t)]=0\). The first factor gives the original point; the other intersection has \(x=-t-1/(2t)\).`,SR`Set this equal to \(-2\): \(t+1/(2t)=2\). Multiplication by \(2t\) gives \(2t^2-4t+1=0\), whose roots are \(1\pm\sqrt2/2\). Both are positive, and their product is \(1-(\sqrt2/2)^2=1/2\).`),
DS('E',SR`Let \(\theta\) be the included angle between sides 5 and 7. The area formula gives \(\tfrac12(5)(7)\sin\theta=35/4\), so \(\sin\theta=1/2\). Since a triangle angle lies between 0 and \(\pi\), the possibilities are \(\pi/6\) and \(5\pi/6\).`,SR`If the third side is \(c\), the cosine rule gives \(c^2=5^2+7^2-2(5)(7)\cos\theta=74-70\cos\theta\). The two cosine values are \(\sqrt3/2\) and \(-\sqrt3/2\).`,SR`Thus the two squared side lengths are \(74-35\sqrt3\) and \(74+35\sqrt3\). Taking the positive square root of the larger gives \(\sqrt{74+35\sqrt3}\). The obtuse included angle produces the longer side.`),
DS('D',SR`To obtain \(x^2\) from the product of \(n\) factors, either select one \(x^2\), in \(n\) ways, or select two \(x\)'s, in \(\binom n2\) ways. Hence \(n+\binom n2=n(n+1)/2=55\). Thus \(n^2+n-110=(n-10)(n+11)=0\), and positivity gives \(n=10\).`,SR`To obtain \(x^4\), the only possible selections of non-constant terms are: four \(x\)'s; one \(x^2\) and two \(x\)'s; or two \(x^2\)'s. No factor can supply two terms at once.`,SR`The respective counts are \(\binom{10}4=210\), \(10\binom92=10\times36=360\), and \(\binom{10}2=45\). Adding the disjoint cases gives \(210+360+45=615\).`),
DS('A',SR`On \((0,1)\) the curve is above the axis; on \((1,a)\), with \(a<4\), it is below. Equal unsigned areas mean that their signed integrals cancel: \(\int_0^a(x^2-5x+4)\,dx=0\).`,SR`Integrate: \(a^3/3-5a^2/2+4a=0\). Since \(a>1\), divide by \(a\), then multiply by 6: \(2a^2-15a+24=0\).`,SR`The quadratic formula gives \(a=(15\pm\sqrt{225-192})/4=(15\pm\sqrt{33})/4\). The plus value exceeds 4 because \(\sqrt{33}>1\). The minus value is between 1 and 4 because \(\sqrt{33}<11\) and \(\sqrt{33}>0\). Only \((15-\sqrt{33})/4\) satisfies the stated interval.`),
DS('E',SR`Set \(t=|x-y|\). Since \(x,y>0\) and \(x+y=6\), we have \(0\le t<6\). The identity \((x+y)^2-(x-y)^2=4xy\) gives \(xy=(36-t^2)/4\).`,SR`The expression is \(F(t)=t(36-t^2)/4=9t-t^3/4\). Its derivative is \(F'(t)=9-3t^2/4\), which vanishes when \(t^2=12\), so \(t=2\sqrt3\). The derivative is positive before this value and negative after it, so this is the global maximum on the interval.`,SR`At this value \(xy=(36-12)/4=6\), and the required product is \(6\times2\sqrt3=12\sqrt3\). It is attained at \(x=3+\sqrt3,y=3-\sqrt3\), or with the variables interchanged; both numbers are positive.`),
DS('B',SR`Move everything to one side and retain the common factor: \((x-2)[(x-1)(x-3)-k]=0\). Since \((x-1)(x-3)=(x-2)^2-1\), this becomes \((x-2)[(x-2)^2-(k+1)]=0\).`,SR`The value \(x=2\) is always a solution. If \(k+1>0\), the second factor gives \(x=2\pm\sqrt{k+1}\), two distinct values different from 2. There are then exactly three distinct real solutions.`,SR`If \(k=-1\), all the factors vanish only at \(x=2\), so multiplicity does not give three distinct solutions. If \(k<-1\), a real square cannot equal \(k+1<0\), leaving only \(x=2\). Thus precisely \(k>-1\) works.`),
DS('C',SR`The denominator \(x^2+1\) is positive for every real \(x\), so multiplication does not lose or introduce roots. Rearranging gives \((1-k)x^2-4x+(5-k)=0\).`,SR`First handle the case where the quadratic coefficient disappears. At \(k=1\), the equation is \(-4x+4=0\), with the unique root \(x=1\).`,SR`For \(k\ne1\), exactly one distinct real root requires discriminant zero: \(16-4(1-k)(5-k)=0\). Expanding \((1-k)(5-k)=5-6k+k^2\) gives \(k^2-6k+1=0\), so \(k=3\pm2\sqrt2\). Neither value is 1.`,SR`There are no further cases: a genuine quadratic with positive discriminant has two roots and with negative discriminant has none. The required sum is \(1+(3+2\sqrt2)+(3-2\sqrt2)=7\).`),
DS('A',SR`Convergence gives \(|r|<1\). The two given sums are \(a/(1-r)=3\) and \(a/(1+r)=1\). Hence \(a=3(1-r)=1+r\), so \(3-3r=1+r\), \(4r=2\), and \(r=1/2\). Then \(a=3/2\).`,SR`Group the required series in blocks of three terms. The first block is \(a(1+r-r^2)\); the next is \(ar^3(1+r-r^2)\); subsequent blocks have ratio \(r^3\). Its sum is therefore \(a(1+r-r^2)/(1-r^3)\).`,SR`Substitute: \(1+r-r^2=1+1/2-1/4=5/4\) and \(1-r^3=1-1/8=7/8\). The sum is \((3/2)(5/4)/(7/8)=(15/8)(8/7)=15/7\). Grouping consecutive finite blocks is valid for this convergent geometric series.`),
DS('D',SR`All terms are positive, so reciprocals are defined. Taking reciprocals of the recurrence gives \(1/a_{n+1}=(a_n+2)/(2a_n)=1/2+1/a_n\). Thus the reciprocals form an arithmetic progression starting at 1 with difference \(1/2\).`,SR`Therefore \(1/a_n=1+(n-1)/2=(n+1)/2\), giving \(a_n=2/(n+1)\). A factor in the product is \([2+2/(n+1)]/[2-2/(n+1)]=(n+2)/n\). Its denominator is non-zero for every positive \(n\).`,SR`The product is \((3/1)(4/2)(5/3)\cdots(32/30)\). The factors 3 through 30 in the numerator cancel with the same factors in the denominator. What remains is \((31\times32)/(1\times2)=496\).`)
];
const DETAILED_SET_A_P2=[
DS('G',SR`Complete the square: \(x^2+ax+b=(x+a/2)^2+b-a^2/4\). The squared term has minimum zero, attained at \(x=-a/2\). Thus the polynomial is strictly positive for all real inputs exactly when \(b-a^2/4>0\), or \(a^2<4b\). This is statement S.`,SR`S also forces \(b>0\), because \(a^2\ge0\). Consequently requiring both R and S is equivalent to requiring S alone, which is equivalent to P. This proves I and II.`,SR`For III, if P holds, evaluate the polynomial at \(x=0\): its value is \(b>0\), which is R. Therefore R is necessary. All three statements are true. The condition Q is not needed.`),
DS('C',SR`To make a statement of the form “for every x, if A then B” false, there must be at least one x for which A is true and B is false. Here A is \(x>0\), so the negation must begin by choosing some positive x.`,SR`For this x, B says there exists a y in \((0,x)\) with \(f(y)>f(x)\). Its negation says that every y in that interval fails the strict inequality, so \(f(y)\le f(x)\).`,SR`Combining the quantifiers gives: there exists \(x>0\) such that every \(y\) with \(0<y<x\) satisfies \(f(y)\le f(x)\). This is C. A wrongly requires this for every x; B checks only one y, which is insufficient to rule out a different successful y.`),
DS('C',SR`Line I follows by subtracting \(b^2\) from \(a^2>b^2\). Line II uses the correct factorisation \(a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\). Neither step changes the condition.`,SR`A positive product means that the two non-zero factors have the same sign. They may both be positive or both negative. Line III wrongly discards the second possibility.`,SR`For an explicit counterexample take \(a=-2,b=1\). Then \(a^2=4>1=b^2\), but \(a-b=-3\) and \(a+b=-1\). Their product is positive even though neither factor is positive, and \(a>b\) is false. The first error is therefore line III.`),
DS('E',SR`For I, an integer is either odd, twice an odd integer, or divisible by 4. The square of an odd integer is odd. If \(m=2r\) with r odd, then \(m^2=4r^2\) is divisible by 4 but not by 8. Thus \(8\) dividing \(m^2\) forces the remaining case, namely \(4\) dividing m. This includes \(m=0\). I is true.`,SR`II is false: choose \(m=3,n=4\). Their highest common factor is 1 and their product is 12, but neither 3 nor 4 is divisible by 12. Coprime factors can carry different prime factors of 12.`,SR`For III, use prime factorisation of \(|m|\), which is positive because \(m\ne0\). If a prime occurs to exponent e in \(|m|\), it occurs to exponent \(2e\) in \(m^2\). Since \(m^2\) divides \(n^2\), the exponent in \(n^2\) is at least \(2e\), so the exponent in \(|n|\) is at least e. Hence every prime power required by \(|m|\) divides \(|n|\), proving m divides n. If \(n=0\), this conclusion is immediate. I and III are true.`),
DS('D',SR`A cyclic quadrilateral is one whose four vertices lie on one circle. The converse of the opposite-angle theorem says that a quadrilateral with opposite angles summing to \(180^\circ\) is cyclic. Thus I is sufficient.`,SR`In II, both \(\angle ABD\) and \(\angle ACD\) stand on the same segment AD. The converse of the equal-angles-in-the-same-segment theorem places A, B, C and D on one circle. Thus II is sufficient.`,SR`III is not sufficient. Take a non-rectangular parallelogram with angles \(60^\circ,120^\circ,60^\circ,120^\circ\). Its opposite sides AB and CD are equal, but its opposite angles A and C sum to \(120^\circ\), not \(180^\circ\); it is not cyclic. Therefore only I and II suffice.`),
DS('D',SR`If the diagonals bisect each other, the quadrilateral is a parallelogram. Write three consecutive vertices as \(A=(0,0),B=(u,0),D=(v,w)\), where \(u>0,w\ne0\); then \(C=(u+v,w)\). The squared diagonal lengths are \((u+v)^2+w^2\) and \((v-u)^2+w^2\).`,SR`Equality of these lengths gives \((u+v)^2=(v-u)^2\), so \(4uv=0\). Since \(u>0\), we get \(v=0\). Thus AD is vertical and AB horizontal, making a right angle. A parallelogram with one right angle is a rectangle. D is sufficient.`,SR`The other choices do not force a rectangle. The kite with vertices \((0,2),(3/2,0),(0,-1),(-3/2,0)\) has equal perpendicular diagonals of length 3, equal adjacent sides at its top, and equal opposite angles at its left and right vertices, but is not a rectangle. This rules out A, B and E.`,SR`For C, an isosceles trapezium with vertices \((-2,0),(2,0),(1,2),(-1,2)\) has equal diagonals of length \(\sqrt{13}\), and its opposite non-parallel sides both have length \(\sqrt5\). It is not a rectangle, so C is not sufficient.`),
DS('C',SR`Apply the given identity first at \(x=0\): \(f(0)+f(2)=0\). Apply it at \(x=2\): \(f(2)+f(0)=4\).`,SR`The left sides are identical because addition is commutative. They cannot equal both 0 and 4. This contradiction rules out every function defined at those inputs, so in particular no function defined on all the reals can satisfy the identity. C is correct.`),
DS('E',SR`For I, choose the constant function \(f(x)=1/2\). Then \(f(x)+f(1-x)=1/2+1/2=1\) for every real x. I is possible.`,SR`For II, substitute \(x=0\) to get \(f(0)+f(1)=0\), and \(x=1\) to get \(f(1)+f(0)=1\). These contradict each other, so II is impossible.`,SR`For III, choose the constant function \(f(x)=1\). The product is \(1\times1=1\) for every input. Each proposed equation is considered independently, so we may use different functions for I and III. Those two statements, and only those, are possible.`),
DS('E',SR`One-to-one means that equal outputs can occur only at equal inputs: \(f(u)=f(v)\) implies \(u=v\). In I this would require \(x=x+1\), which is impossible. Thus I is true.`,SR`In II it gives \(x^2=4\), so \(x=2\) or \(x=-2\). The second possibility disproves the claim that x must be 2. For instance \(f(u)=u\) is one-to-one and works as a counterexample at \(x=-2\).`,SR`In III it gives \(x=-x\), so \(2x=0\) and \(x=0\). Thus III is true. The correct selection is I and III.`),
DS('A',SR`I is true. Two roots of opposite signs are real and unequal: a negative number cannot equal a positive number. Hence R always implies P.`,SR`II is false if the positive roots coincide. The discriminant is \((-2a)^2-4(a+3)=4(a^2-a-3)\). Choose \(a=(1+\sqrt{13})/2>0\), which satisfies \(a^2=a+3\). The equation becomes \((x-a)^2=0\). Its only distinct root is positive, so Q holds but P fails.`,SR`III is false when both distinct roots are negative. Take \(a=-2\), giving \(x^2+4x+1=0\). Its roots are \(-2\pm\sqrt3\), both negative because \(\sqrt3<2\). Thus P holds and Q does not, but R fails. Only I is true.`),
DS('E',SR`Line I is correct: \(n(n-1)(n+1)(n^2+1)=n(n^2-1)(n^2+1)=n(n^4-1)=n^5-n\). Among three consecutive integers there is a multiple of 3 and at least one even integer, so lines II and III are valid.`,SR`Line IV is false. Three consecutive integers need not include a multiple of 5: \(1,2,3\) are an immediate counterexample. Therefore the first error is in IV, regardless of whether the final theorem itself is true.`,SR`The theorem can be repaired without that false claim. If n leaves remainder 0, 1 or 4 on division by 5, one of \(n,n-1,n+1\) is divisible by 5. If n leaves remainder 2 or 3, \(n^2+1\) leaves remainder \(4+1=5\) or \(9+1=10\), also divisible by 5. Together with the established divisibility by 2 and 3, this gives divisibility by 30. A true conclusion does not make the original proof valid.`),
DS('A',SR`Begin with S, which introduces the two odd integers a and b. By the definition of odd, Q then writes them as \(a=2r+1,b=2s+1\), with r and s integers.`,SR`Add these expressions: \(a+b=(2r+1)+(2s+1)=2r+2s+2=2(r+s+1)\). This is step P. Since r and s are integers, \(r+s+1\) is an integer, so this expression is divisible by 2; that is R.`,SR`The required order is therefore S, Q, P, R. It introduces the objects, expresses their assumed property, performs the algebra, and interprets the result.`),
DS('E',SR`For I, \(f(0)=q=2\). Then \(f(1)=1+p+q=3\) gives \(1+p+2=3\), so \(p=0\). Exactly one polynomial, \(x^2+2\), fits. I determines f uniquely.`,SR`For II, \(q=4\). Equal roots require discriminant \(p^2-4q=p^2-16=0\), giving \(p=4\) or \(p=-4\). Both \((x+2)^2\) and \((x-2)^2\) fit, so uniqueness fails.`,SR`For III, expanding \((x-r_1)(x-r_2)\) gives \(x^2-(r_1+r_2)x+r_1r_2\). The given sum and product therefore force \(p=-4,q=3\), giving exactly \(x^2-4x+3\). I and III determine f uniquely.`),
DS('D',SR`For I, an odd integer \(n=2r+1\) has square \(4r^2+4r+1=2(2r^2+2r)+1\), which is odd. Conversely an even integer \(n=2r\) has even square \(4r^2\), so an odd square forces n odd. I is equivalent.`,SR`For II, if \(n=2r+1\), then \(n^2-1=4r(r+1)\). One of the consecutive integers r and r+1 is even, so this product is divisible by 8. Conversely, if n is even, \(n^2-1\) is odd and cannot be divisible by 8. II is equivalent.`,SR`III holds for every integer, not just odd ones: \(n^3-n=n(n-1)(n+1)\) is the product of three consecutive integers, hence contains a factor of 3 and a factor of 2. For example n=2 is even but gives \(8-2=6\). Thus III is not equivalent. The answer is I and II.`),
DS('D',SR`For I there are only two parity categories, even and odd. Six integers cannot all occupy different categories, so at least two have the same parity.`,SR`For II there are only five possible remainders after division by 5: 0,1,2,3,4. Among six integers two have the same remainder. Write them as \(5u+r\) and \(5v+r\); their difference is \(5(u-v)\), a multiple of 5.`,SR`III is false. Choose \(1,6,11,16,21,26\). Each leaves remainder 1 on division by 5, so the sum of any two leaves remainder 2 and is not divisible by 5. These integers are distinct. Thus only I and II must hold.`),
DS('B',SR`Every integer n can be written \(n=3q+r\), where \(r\) is 0,1 or 2. For every positive power j, \(n^j-r^j\) has factor \(n-r=3q\), using \(n^j-r^j=(n-r)(n^{j-1}+n^{j-2}r+\cdots+r^{j-1})\). Thus it is divisible by 3.`,SR`Write \(P(x)=c_0+c_1x+\cdots+c_dx^d\), with integer coefficients. Subtracting P(r) from P(n) gives \(c_1(n-r)+\cdots+c_d(n^d-r^d)\). Every summand is divisible by 3, so P(n) and P(r) have the same remainder.`,SR`By the hypothesis, each of P(0), P(1) and P(2) is divisible by 3. Whichever remainder r belongs to n, P(r) is therefore divisible by 3, and so is P(n). This establishes B for every integer, including negative ones. It does not force individual coefficients to be multiples of 3: \(P(x)=x(x-1)(x-2)\) is a counterexample to that stronger claim.`),
DS('G',SR`The Factor Theorem and degree 4 imply \(f(x)=A x(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)\) for some non-zero constant A. At x=4 its value is \(24A\); at x=-1 it is \(A(-1)(-2)(-3)(-4)=24A\). Thus I holds.`,SR`Substituting 3-x gives \(f(3-x)=A(3-x)(2-x)(1-x)(-x)\). Changing each factor's sign contributes \((-1)^4=1\), leaving the same four factors as f(x), merely in reverse order. Therefore II holds.`,SR`For III set \(u=x-3/2\). Pair symmetric factors to obtain \(f(x)=A(u^2-9/4)(u^2-1/4)=A(u^4-5u^2/2+9/16)\). Differentiating gives \(f'(x)=A(4u^3-5u)\), since u differs from x only by a constant; this also follows by expanding the powers of x. At \(x=3/2\), u=0 and the derivative is zero. All three statements hold.`),
DS('C',SR`A real input x makes \(f(x^2)=0\) precisely when \(x^2\) is a real root of f. A negative root of f contributes no real x; a positive root r contributes the two distinct inputs \(\pm\sqrt r\); a root at zero contributes just x=0.`,SR`If zero is not a root, choose 0,1,2 or 3 of f's three roots positive and the others negative. This produces respectively 0,2,4 or 6 roots of the composition. If zero is a root, choose 0,1 or 2 of the other roots positive. This produces 1,3 or 5 roots.`,SR`All choices can actually occur: take a cubic whose three distinct roots are the chosen values. For counts 0 through 6, example root sets are \(\{-3,-2,-1\},\{0,-2,-1\},\{1,-2,-1\},\{0,1,-1\},\{1,2,-1\},\{0,1,2\},\{1,2,3\}\). Thus every integer from 0 to 6 is possible.`),
DS('A',SR`Let \(r=x+y\), which is rational by hypothesis. If x-y were rational as well, then adding the two rational numbers would make \(2x=(x+y)+(x-y)\) rational, and division by 2 would make x rational. This contradicts the hypothesis, so I is true.`,SR`For II and III, use \(x=\sqrt2,y=-\sqrt2\). Both numbers are irrational and their sum is 0, which is rational. But their product is -2 and their quotient is -1, both rational. Thus II and III need not hold.`,SR`Only I is forced. The quotient is defined because an irrational number cannot equal the rational number zero.`),
DS('C',SR`If P holds, then for every x the value \(t=f(x)\) is non-negative. Consequently \(|t|=t\), so \(t|t|=t^2\). Thus P implies Q: P is sufficient.`,SR`Conversely suppose Q holds. If some value t=f(x) were negative, then \(|t|=-t\) and the equation would give \(t^2=-t^2\). Hence \(2t^2=0\), which forces t=0, contradicting t<0. No value can be negative, so P holds.`,SR`Thus Q implies P as well: P is necessary. Zero causes no exception because both sides of the equality are zero there. P is both necessary and sufficient for Q.`)
];
const DETAILED_SET_B_P1=[
DS('A',SR`The given division means \(P(x)=(x-1)(x-2)H(x)+2x+1\) for some polynomial H. Substituting x=1 and x=2 removes the unknown multiple, giving \(P(1)=3\) and \(P(2)=5\).`,SR`For \(Q(x)=P(3-x)+xP(x)\), calculate \(Q(1)=P(2)+P(1)=5+3=8\), and \(Q(2)=P(1)+2P(2)=3+10=13\).`,SR`A remainder on division by a quadratic must have degree less than 2. Write it as \(ax+b\). Its values at 1 and 2 must match Q, so \(a+b=8\) and \(2a+b=13\). Subtraction gives a=5, and then b=3. The answer is \(5x+3\); the quadratic distractor is not a permitted remainder even though it agrees at the two roots.`),
DS('B',SR`Let \(a_j\) be day j's output and \(S=a_1+\cdots+a_n\). We know \(a_1=5\) and \(a_{j+1}=2a_j-3\). Add the recurrence for \(j=1,2,\ldots,n-1\). The left side is \(a_2+\cdots+a_n=S-5\); the right side is \(2(a_1+\cdots+a_{n-1})-3(n-1)=2(S-a_n)-3(n-1)\).`,SR`Thus \(S-5=2S-2a_n-3n+3\). Move terms to obtain \(S=2a_n+3n-8\). The problem also says \(S=2a_n+16\), so \(3n-8=16\), \(3n=24\), and \(n=8\).`,SR`For a numerical check, the first eight outputs are \(5,7,11,19,35,67,131,259\). Their sum is 534, and \(2(259)+16=534\). Hence the inferred day count satisfies the original condition.`),
DS('E',SR`Write \(F(x)=c_0+c_1x+c_2x^2+\cdots+c_6x^6=(1+2x)^6\). At \(x=1/2\), the even-power terms are exactly \(c_0+c_2/4+c_4/16+c_6/64\), the expression we need.`,SR`At \(x=-1/2\), those even-power terms are unchanged, while every odd-power term changes sign. Therefore \(F(1/2)+F(-1/2)\) equals twice the required expression.`,SR`Evaluate the factored form: \(F(1/2)=(1+1)^6=64\), and \(F(-1/2)=(1-1)^6=0\). Dividing their sum by 2 gives \((64+0)/2=32\).`),
DS('E',SR`Let \(f(x)=(x-1)^2-4\). A right translation by 3 gives \(f(x-3)\). A horizontal compression with scale factor \(1/2\) replaces the input x by 2x. For G, apply this to the already translated expression: \(G(x)=f(2x-3)=(2x-4)^2-4\).`,SR`For H, compression first gives \(f(2x)\). Translating this right by 3 replaces x by x-3, giving \(H(x)=f(2(x-3))=(2x-7)^2-4\). The order matters because the translation is also scaled in the second expression.`,SR`At an intersection \((2x-4)^2=(2x-7)^2\). Subtract and factor: \([(2x-4)-(2x-7)][(2x-4)+(2x-7)]=3(4x-11)=0\). Thus the unique intersection has \(x=11/4\).`),
DS('E',SR`Stopping on the third draw means that both colours have appeared by draw three, but not by draw two. Hence the first two colours must agree and the third must be the other colour. The only qualifying colour sequences are RRB and BBR.`,SR`For RRB, multiply the probabilities as the bag changes: \(P(\mathrm{RRB})=(3/5)(2/4)(2/3)=12/60=1/5\). For BBR, \(P(\mathrm{BBR})=(2/5)(1/4)(3/3)=1/10\). The events are disjoint, so the total probability of stopping on draw three is \(1/5+1/10=3/10\).`,SR`Within that stopping event, starting red occurs only in RRB. Therefore the conditional probability is \((1/5)/(3/10)=(1/5)(10/3)=2/3\). A sequence such as RBR is excluded because it would already have stopped on draw two.`),
DS('B',SR`Since \(\sin x\cos x=-3/8\ne0\), neither sine nor cosine is zero. Put \(t=\tan x=\sin x/\cos x\). From \(\sin^2x+\cos^2x=1\), division by \(\cos^2x\) gives \(1+t^2=1/\cos^2x\).`,SR`Thus \(\sin x\cos x=t\cos^2x=t/(1+t^2)\). The given equation becomes \(t/(1+t^2)=-3/8\). Multiply by \(8(1+t^2)>0\): \(8t=-3-3t^2\), so \(3t^2+8t+3=0\).`,SR`The quadratic formula gives \(t=(-8\pm\sqrt{64-36})/6=(-4\pm\sqrt7)/3\). Both values are negative because \(\sqrt7<4\). Each corresponds to exactly one angle in the second quadrant of \((0,\pi)\), where the given sine-cosine product is negative.`,SR`The larger value minus the smaller is \([(-4+\sqrt7)-(-4-\sqrt7)]/3=2\sqrt7/3\).`),
DS('D',SR`Set \(u=\log_2x\). The first outer logarithm requires \(u>0\). The second inner logarithm is \(\log_2(16/x)=4-u\), and the second outer logarithm requires \(4-u>0\). Together, the domain is \(0<u<4\).`,SR`Within this domain the sum of logarithms is \(\log_2(u(4-u))\). Since base 2 is greater than 1, \(\log_2(u(4-u))>1\) is equivalent to \(u(4-u)>2\).`,SR`Expand and complete the square: \(4u-u^2>2\) means \(u^2-4u+2<0\), or \((u-2)^2<2\). Hence \(2-\sqrt2<u<2+\sqrt2\). Both endpoints are inside \((0,4)\), so the logarithm domain removes nothing further.`,SR`Exponentiation with base 2 preserves strict order. Since \(x=2^u\), the answer is \(2^{2-\sqrt2}<x<2^{2+\sqrt2}\). Neither endpoint is included because the original inequality is strict.`),
DS('B',SR`Place \(A=(0,0)\) and \(B=(8,0)\). The side AC has length 6 and makes angle \(60^\circ\) with AB, so \(C=(6\cos60^\circ,6\sin60^\circ)=(3,3\sqrt3)\). The midpoint is \(P=(4,0)\), and \(AQ=2QC\) gives \(Q=(2,2\sqrt3)\).`,SR`A point a fraction t of the way from B to Q is \((8-6t,2\sqrt3t)\). A point a fraction s of the way from C to P is \((3+s,3\sqrt3(1-s))\). At R these agree, so \(8-6t=3+s\) and \(2t=3(1-s)\).`,SR`The first equation gives \(s=5-6t\). Substitute into the second: \(2t=3(1-5+6t)=-12+18t\). Thus \(16t=12\), \(t=3/4\), and \(s=1/2\). R is therefore three quarters of the way from B to Q, making \(RQ=BQ/4\).`,SR`Triangles RQC and BQC share the base QC; since Q lies on AC, R's perpendicular height above AC is one quarter of B's. Thus their areas have ratio 1:4. Also BQC has one third of the area of ABC, because \(QC=AC/3\) and they share B's height.`,SR`The area of ABC is \(\tfrac12(8)(6)\sin60^\circ=12\sqrt3\). The required area is \((1/4)(1/3)(12\sqrt3)=\sqrt3\).`),
DS('B',SR`Write the quadratic as \(f(x)=ax^2+bx+c\). Its values at 0,1,2 are \(c,a+b+c,4a+2b+c\). One trapezium of width 2 gives estimate \(2[f(0)+f(2)]/2=4a+2b+2c=14\).`,SR`Two trapezia of width 1 give \([f(0)+2f(1)+f(2)]/2=3a+2b+2c=12\). Subtracting this equation from the first gives a=2. Substitution in the first then gives \(8+2b+2c=14\), so \(2b+2c=6\).`,SR`The exact integral is \([ax^3/3+bx^2/2+cx]_0^2=8a/3+2b+2c\). Substitute the quantities already determined: \(16/3+6=34/3\). We do not need to determine b and c separately because the integral uses only their known combination.`),
DS('E',SR`Subtract \(x^2+y^2=25\) from \((x-6)^2+y^2=25\). Expanding gives \(-12x+36=0\), so both intersections have x=3. Substitution gives \(y^2=25-9=16\); take \(A=(3,4),B=(3,-4)\).`,SR`The radius OA has slope \(4/3\), so its perpendicular tangent has slope \(-3/4\). Through A this gives \(y-4=-3(x-3)/4\), or \(3x+4y=25\). The tangent at B similarly has equation \(3x-4y=25\).`,SR`Subtract the tangent equations to obtain y=0; then \(3x=25\). Thus \(T=(25/3,0)\). The diagonal OT has length \(25/3\). Points A and B lie respectively 4 units above and below this diagonal.`,SR`Split the quadrilateral along OT into triangles OAT and OBT. Each has area \(\tfrac12(25/3)(4)=50/3\), so the total area is \(100/3\).`),
DS('A',SR`If the first cyclist travels for total time T, the distance is \(uT/2+vT/2\). Dividing by T gives average speed \((u+v)/2\).`,SR`If the second travels total distance d, the total time is \((d/2)/u+(d/2)/v=d(u+v)/(2uv)\). Dividing d by this time gives average speed \(2uv/(u+v)\).`,SR`The ratio condition is \([(u+v)/2]/[2uv/(u+v)]=9/8\), so \((u+v)^2/(4uv)=9/8\). Multiplying by \(8uv\) gives \(2(u+v)^2=9uv\).`,SR`Put \(r=u/v\), using \(v>0\). Divide by \(v^2\): \(2(r+1)^2=9r\), or \(2r^2-5r+2=(2r-1)(r-2)=0\). The roots are 1/2 and 2. Since u>v, r>1, so \(u/v=2\).`),
DS('F',SR`Translate the origin to the centre of rotation: put \(u=x-2\), \(v=y-5\). The translated polynomial still has leading coefficient 1, so write \(v=u^3+pu^2+qu+r\). A half-turn sends \((u,v)\) to \((-u,-v)\).`,SR`For this to leave the graph unchanged, substituting -u must negate the value. Comparing \(-u^3+pu^2-qu+r\) with \(-u^3-pu^2-qu-r\) gives \(p=-p\) and \(r=-r\), so \(p=r=0\). Thus \(y=(x-2)^3+q(x-2)+5\).`,SR`Expand before differentiating if desired: \(y=x^3-6x^2+(12+q)x+(1-2q)\). Hence \(y'=3x^2-12x+12+q=3(x-2)^2+q\). At x=1 the gradient is \(3+q=-3\), so q=-6.`,SR`The stationary equation is \(3(x-2)^2-6=0\), giving \(x=2\pm\sqrt2\). The derivative is negative between these points and positive to the right of both, so the right-hand point is the local minimum.`,SR`There \(u=\sqrt2\), and \(y=u^3-6u+5=2\sqrt2-6\sqrt2+5=5-4\sqrt2\). The other sign gives the local maximum, not the requested minimum.`),
DS('C',SR`At an input t the curve has ordinate \(t^3-3t\) and derivative \(3t^2-3\). Its tangent is \(y-(t^3-3t)=(3t^2-3)(x-t)\). At x=0 its intercept is \(t^3-3t-t(3t^2-3)=-2t^3\).`,SR`If two tangents at distinct inputs s and t are parallel, their gradients satisfy \(3s^2-3=3t^2-3\), giving \(s^2=t^2\). Since s and t are distinct, they are opposites. Write them as t and -t with t>0.`,SR`Their intercepts are \(-2t^3\) and \(2t^3\), so their vertical separation is \(4t^3=32\). Hence \(t^3=8\), t=2. The common gradient is \(3(2)^2-3=9\).`),
DS('C',SR`The nth term is the difference of successive partial sums: \(a_n=S_n-S_{n-1}\). First divide \(n^2\) by n+1: \(n^2=(n+1)(n-1)+1\), giving \(S_n=n-1+1/(n+1)\).`,SR`For n at least 2, \(S_{n-1}=n-2+1/n\). Subtract to get \(a_n=1+1/(n+1)-1/n=1-1/[n(n+1)]\). At n=1 this same formula gives 1/2, agreeing with \(S_1\), so it holds for every positive n.`,SR`The condition becomes \(1-1/[n(n+1)]>99/100\), hence \(1/[n(n+1)]<1/100\). Both denominators are positive, so \(n(n+1)>100\).`,SR`At n=9 the product is 90; at n=10 it is 110. It increases thereafter, and all smaller positive n give at most 90. Therefore the least n is 10.`),
DS('C',SR`Reflect B=(1,4) across the y-axis to \(B'=(-1,4)\). Because Q lies on that axis, \(QB=QB'\). Next reflect both Q and B' across the x-axis to Q' and \(B''=(-1,-4)\). Because P lies on the x-axis, \(PQ=PQ'\), and reflection also gives \(QB'=Q'B''\).`,SR`Thus every permitted path has the same length as \(AP+PQ'+Q'B''\). A broken path from A to B'' cannot be shorter than their straight-line distance. This distance is \(\sqrt{(4-(-1))^2+(1-(-4))^2}=\sqrt{25+25}=5\sqrt2\).`,SR`We must check that the straight path corresponds to permitted P and Q. The line from (4,1) to (-1,-4) is \(y=x-3\). It crosses the x-axis at \(P=(3,0)\), then the negative y-axis at \(Q'=(0,-3)\). Reflecting back gives \(Q=(0,3)\), on the positive y-axis as required.`,SR`The actual segment lengths are \(AP=\sqrt2\), \(PQ=\sqrt{18}=3\sqrt2\), and \(QB=\sqrt2\). Their sum is \(5\sqrt2\), so the bound is attained and is the minimum.`),
DS('A',SR`There are \(\binom83=8\cdot7\cdot6/(3\cdot2\cdot1)=56\) equally likely selections. For a valid selection, look clockwise at the three gaps of unchosen positions separating the chosen positions. Each gap must contain at least one position; their total is \(8-3=5\).`,SR`Subtract one from each gap. The remaining three non-negative numbers sum to 2. Their possibilities are permutations of (2,0,0), three orders, and permutations of (1,1,0), another three orders. Thus there are six ordered gap patterns.`,SR`Choose one of the eight labelled positions as a starting chosen point, then one of the six gap patterns. This makes \(8\times6=48\) descriptions. Every selection has been described three times, once starting at each of its chosen points. Therefore the number of valid selections is \(48/3=16\).`,SR`The probability is \(16/56=2/7\). Treating positions 8 and 1 as non-adjacent would count selections that are invalid on the circle.`),
DS('D',SR`The translated function is \(g(x)=f(x-2)+3\). The vertical shift contributes \(\int_{-3}^3 3\,dx=3(3-(-3))=18\). For the f part, shifting the horizontal coordinate by 2 takes endpoints -3 and 3 to -5 and 1. Thus \(\int_{-3}^3g(x)\,dx=18+\int_{-5}^1f(u)\,du\).`,SR`For an odd function, reflected intervals have opposite signed integrals: \(\int_{-b}^{-a}f(u)\,du=-\int_a^bf(u)\,du\). Hence \(\int_{-5}^{-2}f= -\int_2^5f=-(-4)=4\).`,SR`Similarly, reflecting [-1,2] gives [-2,1], so \(\int_{-2}^1f=-\int_{-1}^2f=-3\). These intervals join to [-5,1], whose integral is \(4-3=1\). Add the vertical-shift contribution to obtain \(18+1=19\).`),
DS('C',SR`Split at the absolute-value corners. For \(x\le1\), the left side is \((1-x)+(4-x)=5-2x\). For \(1\le x\le4\), it is \((x-1)+(4-x)=3\). For \(x\ge4\), it is \((x-1)+(x-4)=2x-5\).`,SR`On the first branch, \(5-2x=kx\) gives \(x=5/(k+2)\), valid when \(5/(k+2)\le1\). Since k>0, this is k at least 3. On the middle branch, \(3=kx\) gives \(x=3/k\), valid when \(1\le3/k\le4\), or \(3/4\le k\le3\).`,SR`On the final branch, \((2-k)x=5\). A root with x at least 4 requires k<2 and \(5/(2-k)\ge4\), which rearranges to k at least 3/4. Thus this branch contributes for \(3/4\le k<2\). At k=2 its equation becomes 0=5, so contributes nothing.`,SR`For \(0<k<3/4\), there are no roots. At k=3/4, the middle and final roots coincide at x=4, giving just one distinct root. For \(3/4<k<2\), they are different, giving two. For \(2\le k<3\), only the middle branch contributes. At k=3, the first and middle roots coincide at x=1. For k>3, only the first branch contributes.`,SR`Exactly one distinct root therefore occurs when \(k=3/4\) or \(k\ge2\). Counting a corner on both adjacent branches twice would give an incorrect answer.`),
DS('C',SR`Set the two expressions for y equal: \(x+c=(x+1)/(x-1)\), where x cannot be 1. Multiplication gives \((x+c)(x-1)=x+1\), hence \(x^2+(c-2)x-(c+1)=0\).`,SR`The discriminant is \((c-2)^2+4(c+1)=c^2+8>0\). The two roots are \([2-c\pm\sqrt{c^2+8}]/2\), so their horizontal separation is \(\sqrt{c^2+8}\). Substituting x=1 into the quadratic gives -2, so neither root is the forbidden value.`,SR`Both points lie on a line of slope 1, so the difference between their y-coordinates equals the difference between their x-coordinates. By Pythagoras, their distance is \(\sqrt{(c^2+8)+(c^2+8)}=\sqrt{2c^2+16}\).`,SR`Since \(c^2\ge0\), this is at least 4, with equality at c=0. At c=0 the horizontal roots are \(1\pm\sqrt2\), both valid, so the minimum is attained.`),
DS('D',SR`Complete the square inside the outer square: \(x^2-4x=(x-2)^2-4\). Put u=x-2. The intersection equation is \((u^2-4)^2=k\). Four distinct real roots require \(0<k<16\): k=0 merges the two quadratic equations, while k at least 16 prevents four distinct roots.`,SR`Taking square roots gives \(u^2=4+\sqrt k\) or \(u^2=4-\sqrt k\). Write \(a=\sqrt{4+\sqrt k}\) and \(b=\sqrt{4-\sqrt k}\). Then \(a>b>0\) and the four shifted roots are \(-a,-b,b,a\). Translating by 2 does not change their gaps.`,SR`The consecutive gaps are \(a-b,2b,a-b\). For an arithmetic progression they must agree, so \(a-b=2b\), giving a=3b. Square both positive quantities: \(4+\sqrt k=9(4-\sqrt k)\).`,SR`Expanding gives \(4+\sqrt k=36-9\sqrt k\), so \(10\sqrt k=32\) and \(k=(16/5)^2=256/25\). This lies between 0 and 16. Indeed b=\(2/\sqrt5\) and a=\(6/\sqrt5\), giving four roots separated by \(4/\sqrt5\).`)
];
const DETAILED_SET_B_P2=[
DS('D',SR`For I, suppose neither x nor y exceeded 1. Then \(x\le1\) and \(y\le1\), giving \(x+y\le2\). This contradicts the hypothesis \(x+y>2\). Therefore at least one exceeds 1, so I holds.`,SR`For II, expand the non-negative square: \((x-y)^2\ge0\) gives \(x^2+y^2\ge2xy\), hence \((x+y)^2\ge4xy\). If xy>1, then \((x+y)^2>4\). Because x+y is positive, taking the positive square root gives x+y>2. Thus II holds.`,SR`III fails at \(x=3,y=1/4\). Their sum is \(13/4>2\), but their product is \(3/4<1\). The converse of II is therefore not automatic. I and II, but not III, are true.`),
DS('C',SR`Call A the assertion that every term is positive, and B the assertion that every partial sum is positive. The contrapositive of “if A then B” is “if not B then not A”. It reverses the direction and negates both assertions.`,SR`The negation of “every partial sum is positive” is “some partial sum is non-positive”. Zero must be included: non-positive means at most zero, not strictly negative. Similarly the negation of “every term is positive” is “some term is non-positive”.`,SR`Therefore the contrapositive is: if some partial sum is non-positive, then some term is non-positive. This is C. A is the inverse, B is the converse, and D–F strengthen or otherwise change one of the required negations. Neither a negation nor a contrapositive is obtained by replacing “every” with “every negative”.`),
DS('D',SR`A counterexample must satisfy both hypotheses and make at least one part of the conclusion false. For D, \((a,b,c,d)=(4,4,5,2)\). The sums are 8 and 7, so \(a+b>c+d\). The products are 16 and 10, so \(ab>cd\). But a>c says 4>5, which is false.`,SR`For A, the sums are 9 and 5, products 20 and 6, and both 5>3 and 4>2 hold. For B, the sums are 8 and 6, products 12 and 5, and both 6>5 and 2>1 hold. For C, the sums are 8 and 6, products 15 and 8, and both 3>2 and 5>4 hold. These three examples support the conclusion rather than refute it.`,SR`For E, the sums are both 10 and the products are 21 and 24. Neither strict hypothesis holds, so E cannot refute the implication, even though part of its conclusion fails. D is the unique counterexample.`),
DS('F',SR`Line 1 is valid: the left side \(|x-2|\) is non-negative, so it cannot be strictly less than x+1 unless x+1>0.`,SR`Under that necessary sign condition, squaring preserves the strict comparison because both sides are non-negative. Expansion of line 2 gives \(x^2-4x+4<x^2+2x+1\), so \(-6x+3<0\), giving \(x>1/2\) as stated in line 3.`,SR`For every x>1/2, we have x+1>3/2>0, so line 4 is valid. Also the squared inequality holds for all such x by reversing the algebra above. Taking non-negative square roots then gives \(|x-2|<x+1\), proving sufficiency.`,SR`Thus no extraneous values remain, and every line is justified. F, “There is no error”, is correct. Squaring is not automatically wrong; the relevant issue is whether its sign conditions and converse are checked.`),
DS('B',SR`P starts from \(0<a<b\). Multiplication of a<b by positive a gives \(a^2<ab\); multiplication by positive b gives \(ab<b^2\). These combine as \(a^2<ab<b^2\).`,SR`Next S adds ab to all three parts, giving \(a^2+ab<2ab<ab+b^2\). Factor the outer expressions: \(a(a+b)<2ab<b(a+b)\).`,SR`Q divides this last inequality by \(a+b>0\), preserving both inequality directions. The result is \(a<2ab/(a+b)<b\), which R states. Hence the order is P,S,Q,R. Putting Q immediately after P would divide the wrong displayed inequality.`),
DS('E',SR`For any x between 0 and 1, non-decreasing behaviour gives \(f(0)\le f(x)\le f(1)\). Both endpoint values equal 7, so \(7\le f(x)\le7\), forcing \(f(x)=7\). Including the endpoints proves I.`,SR`The integral in III is consequently \(\int_0^1 7\,dx=[7x]_0^1=7\). Hence III holds.`,SR`II is not forced outside that interval. Define f(x)=7 for x at most 1, and f(x)=x+6 for x greater than 1. It is constant up to 1, then increases from 7, so it is non-decreasing everywhere. It has the stated endpoint values but, for example, f(2)=8. Thus II fails, and the answer is I and III.`),
DS('G',SR`The three roots and positive leading coefficient give \(f(x)=A(x-a)(x-b)(x-c)\), where A>0 and a<b<c. To find the derivative at a, put x=a+h and expand to first order: \(f(a+h)=Ah(a-b+h)(a-c+h)\). The coefficient of h is \(A(a-b)(a-c)\), which is positive because both differences are negative. Thus I holds.`,SR`At b, put x=b+h. The coefficient of h is \(A(b-a)(b-c)\). The first difference is positive and the second negative, so \(f'(b)<0\), proving II. These coefficient calculations are simply differentiation of the expanded cubic at h=0.`,SR`For III, write x=c+t with t>0. Expanding gives \(f(c+t)=A[t^3+(2c-a-b)t^2+(c-a)(c-b)t]\). Differentiating the powers gives \(f'(c+t)=A[3t^2+2(2c-a-b)t+(c-a)(c-b)]\).`,SR`Every term in this bracket is positive: t>0, \(2c-a-b=(c-a)+(c-b)>0\), and both c-a and c-b are positive. Therefore III holds throughout x>c. All three statements are true.`),
DS('C',SR`Any common divisor of n and n+1 divides their difference 1, so the fraction is already in lowest terms. A reduced fraction can equal \(m/10^r\) only if its denominator divides \(10^r=2^r5^r\). Thus the only allowed prime factors of n+1 are 2 and 5.`,SR`Conversely, if \(n+1=2^a5^b\), choose r at least both a and b. Then \(10^r/(n+1)\) is an integer, and multiplying numerator and denominator by it produces a denominator \(10^r\). Thus this prime-factor condition is also sufficient.`,SR`List the numbers from 2 to 20 having only these factors: \(2,4,5,8,10,16,20\). They correspond to \(n=1,3,4,7,9,15,19\), seven values. Denominator 1 would give n=0, outside the range.`),
DS('F',SR`Suppose A's label is true, so gold is in B. The remaining contents, silver and empty, must occupy A and C. If silver is in C, then A is empty: B's and C's labels are also true, giving three true labels. If C is empty, A contains silver: B's and C's labels are false, giving only one true label. Neither case gives two.`,SR`Now suppose A's label is false. To have exactly two true labels, both B's and C's must be true. Thus C contains silver and A is empty. The only remaining box for gold is B, but that makes A's label true, a contradiction.`,SR`These cases exhaust whether the first label is true or false. No assignment of the three contents satisfies the conditions. Therefore F is correct: the conditions are inconsistent, rather than merely insufficient to locate the gold.`),
DS('D',SR`Line 1 follows from the Factor Theorem: roots 0 and 1 give factors x and x-1, so \(f(x)=x(x-1)g(x)\). Lines 2 and 3 correctly observe that \(x\ge0\), \(x-1\le0\), and hence their product is non-positive on [0,1].`,SR`Line 4 multiplies that non-positive product by g(x) without knowing g(x)'s sign. A negative g(x) reverses the sign, so this inference is invalid. For example take the constant polynomial g(x)=-1, giving \(f(x)=x(1-x)\).`,SR`This example satisfies f(0)=f(1)=0, yet its integral is \(\int_0^1(x-x^2)\,dx=[x^2/2-x^3/3]_0^1=1/2-1/3=1/6>0\). Thus the claim itself fails. Line 5 would be legitimate if line 4 were true; the first error is line 4.`),
DS('E',SR`For I, take any fixed t. We are allowed to choose x after t has been given. Choosing x=t makes \(f_t(x)=(t-t)^2-1=-1<0\). Thus I holds for every t.`,SR`For II, x must be fixed first and then work for every t. Given any proposed x, choose t=x+2. The function value is \((x-(x+2))^2-1=4-1=3>0\). This disproves II for every possible choice of x.`,SR`For III, take any fixed x and choose t=x. The value is again -1, so III holds. The distinction is whether the chosen value may depend on the other variable. I and III permit such a choice; II requires one common x for all parameters.`),
DS('D',SR`Q introduces p=ab. Since a+b=1, expanding \((a+b)^2\) gives \(a^2+b^2=1-2p\), and \((1+a)(1+b)=1+a+b+ab=2+p>0\).`,SR`P follows from \((a-b)^2=(a+b)^2-4ab=1-4p\ge0\), so \(p\le1/4\).`,SR`For R, use a common denominator: \(a/(1+b)+b/(1+a)=[a(1+a)+b(1+b)]/[(1+a)(1+b)]\). The numerator is \(a+b+a^2+b^2=1+(1-2p)=2-2p\), giving the displayed fraction.`,SR`For S, subtract \(2/3\): \((2-2p)/(2+p)-2/3=[3(2-2p)-2(2+p)]/[3(2+p)]=(2-8p)/[3(2+p)]\). P makes its numerator non-negative; Q makes its denominator positive. Hence T concludes the required lower bound.`,SR`The listed valid order is Q,P,R,S,T. Other valid mathematical presentations are possible, but among the given choices the alternatives either use p before defining it, subtract from an expression before obtaining it, or conclude the sign before establishing its bound.`),
DS('D',SR`Arrange the five integers in increasing order as a,b,4,d,e, because the median is 4. Mean 4 means their sum is \(5\times4=20\).`,SR`They are distinct and non-negative, so \(a\ge0\), \(b\ge1\), and \(d\ge5\). Therefore \(e=20-a-b-4-d\le20-0-1-4-5=10\). This proves that 10 is an upper bound.`,SR`To see whether equality is possible, take the five values \(0,1,4,5,10\). They are distinct non-negative integers; their sum is 20, so their mean is 4; their middle value is 4. The largest is 10. The claim is true and its bound is attained, giving D.`),
DS('A',SR`An everywhere-positive derivative is a sufficient condition for a polynomial to be strictly increasing: its graph rises throughout the real line. Thus P implies Q.`,SR`The converse fails for \(f(x)=x^3\). Its derivative is \(3x^2\), which equals zero at x=0, so P is false. Nevertheless f is strictly increasing. To verify this without relying on the derivative at zero, take any u<v and factor \(v^3-u^3=(v-u)(v^2+uv+u^2)\).`,SR`The first factor is positive. The second equals \((v+u/2)^2+3u^2/4\), which is non-negative and can equal zero only when u=v=0. That cannot happen when u<v, so the second factor is positive too. Hence \(v^3>u^3\), proving Q. P is sufficient but not necessary.`),
DS('F',SR`There are six equally likely results. A contains three results, B contains three, and C contains four. Therefore their probabilities are \(1/2,1/2,2/3\), respectively.`,SR`For I, the only result belonging to both A and B is 2. The probability of both is \(1/6\), while the product of their probabilities is \((1/2)(1/2)=1/4\). These differ, so A and B are not independent.`,SR`For II, the shared results of A and C are 1 and 2. The probability of both is \(2/6=1/3\), equal to \((1/2)(2/3)=1/3\). Thus A and C are independent.`,SR`For III, the shared results of B and C are 2 and 4, again giving \(2/6=1/3\), equal to the required product. Hence B and C are independent. Only II and III are true.`),
DS('A',SR`For x>0, both x and 1/x are positive. If x is at least 1, their absolute difference is \(x-1/x\), strictly less than \(x+1/x\) because 1/x>0. If x<1, it is \(1/x-x\), also strictly less than the sum because x>0. Thus c=1 always works, proving I, but equality never occurs, disproving III.`,SR`For II, any c<0 already fails at x=1: the left side is zero and the right side is 2c<0. Now consider \(0\le c<1\). Choose x>1 with \(x^2>(1+c)/(1-c)\); this is possible because the right side is a finite positive number.`,SR`Multiplying by \(1-c>0\) gives \((1-c)x^2>1+c\). Rearrange as \(x^2-1>c(x^2+1)\), then divide by x>0 to obtain \(x-1/x>c(x+1/x)\). Because x>1 the left side is the required absolute value, so this violates the proposed bound.`,SR`Every c<1 therefore fails somewhere. The least valid constant is 1, although its inequality is always strict. Only I is true.`),
DS('G',SR`The recurrence has fixed value 2 because \(3-2/2=2\). Subtract 2 from both sides: \(a_{n+1}-2=1-a_n/2=-(a_n-2)/2\). Thus the deviations from 2 form a geometric sequence with first term -2 and ratio -1/2.`,SR`Hence \(a_n-2=-2(-1/2)^{n-1}\). For even n, n-1 is odd and the power is negative, so the deviation is positive. This proves I.`,SR`For odd n the deviations are negative, starting at -2 and decreasing in magnitude, so those terms lie in [0,2). For even n the deviations are positive, starting at 1 and decreasing in magnitude, so those terms lie in (2,3]. This proves II.`,SR`Taking absolute values in the recurrence for deviations gives \(|a_{n+1}-2|=|a_n-2|/2\), exactly III. Therefore all three statements hold.`),
DS('E',SR`Expand the compositions: \(f(g(x))=2(ax+b)+1=2ax+2b+1\), and \(g(f(x))=a(2x+1)+b=2ax+a+b\). Equality for every x requires \(2b+1=a+b\), or \(b=a-1\).`,SR`For I, \(g(-1)=-a+b=-a+(a-1)=-1\), so it is forced. For II, choose a=-1 and b=-2, which satisfy b=a-1. Then \(g(x)=-x-2\) is decreasing, not increasing. Thus II is false.`,SR`For III, start with \(f(f(g(x)))\). The inner \(f(g(x))\) may be replaced by \(g(f(x))\), giving \(f(g(f(x)))\). Apply the same given identity with input f(x) to get \(g(f(f(x)))\). This is the required equality. I and III hold.`),
DS('C',SR`Put \(u=x-a\) and \(v=x-b\), so \(u+v=2x-a-b\). The equation is \(|u|+|v|=|u+v|\).`,SR`If u and v are both non-negative, both sides are u+v. If both are non-positive, both sides are -u-v. If one is zero, equality also holds. If instead one is strictly positive and the other strictly negative, let their positive magnitudes be p and q. The left side is p+q, while the right side is \(|p-q|\), strictly smaller because both p and q are positive.`,SR`Since \(a<b\), the opposite-sign case occurs exactly for \(a<x<b\): then \(x-a>0\) and \(x-b<0\). Outside this open interval the signs agree, allowing a zero at the endpoints. Therefore equality holds exactly when \(x\le a\) or \(x\ge b\).`),
DS('B',SR`For I, P(0)=1 regardless of k, so it provides no restriction. Choose k=7. Then P(1)=9 is a square as well, but \(P(2)=4+14+1=19\), between \(4^2=16\) and \(5^2=25\). Thus I is false.`,SR`For II, the two tested values are \(P(-1)=2-k\) and \(P(1)=2+k\). Their sum is 4. Non-negative integer squares at most 4 are 0,1,4. The only two that sum to 4 are 0 and 4, in either order. Thus \(k=2\) or \(k=-2\).`,SR`If k=2, \(P(n)=n^2+2n+1=(n+1)^2\). If k=-2, \(P(n)=(n-1)^2\). Each is an integer square for every integer n, so II is true.`,SR`To find a counterexample to III systematically, write \(P(1)=m^2\), giving \(k=m^2-2\). Then \(P(2)=4+2k+1=2m^2+1\), which must also be square. For \(m=1,2,\ldots,12\), these values are \(3,9,19,33,51,73,99,129,163,201,243,289\). The case m=2 gives the already understood k=2; m=12 gives the new square \(289=17^2\).`,SR`Take m=12, hence k=142. Now \(P(1)=144=12^2\), \(P(2)=289=17^2\), but \(P(3)=9+426+1=436\), strictly between \(20^2=400\) and \(21^2=441\). This disproves III. Only II is true.`)
];
